  <running_head>I. IACOMOTI</running_head>
  <bottom_margin>
    <catchword>Pellit</catchword>
  </bottom_margin>
  <main_text>
    <p>
    <i>Quod te nunc opus occupat?</i><br/>
    <i>Quod tractas vigili mente negotium?</i><br/>
    <i>Nam te mollis inertia</i><br/>
    <i>Non unquam potuit fallere, nec leuis</i><br/>
    <i>Somni languidior mora.</i><br/>
    <i>Sed te nobilior cura laboribus</i><br/>
    <i>Exercere bonis solet:</i><br/>
    <i>Seu qua magniloquus non facili via</i><br/>
    <i>Olim dißerunt scholis</i><br/>
    <i>Stagirita suis quaerere te iuuat,</i><br/>
    <i>Atque ingens Anyti reus:</i><br/>
    <i>Qui risum simulans Cecropia improbos</i><br/>
    <i>Gentis carpsit acerbius</i><br/>
    <i>Mores, qui vitium tollere dum studet</i><br/>
    <i>Occultum, inuidia obrutus</i><br/>
    <i>Tandem lethiferis morsibus occidit.</i><br/>
    <i>Seu tu Mincigena potens</i><br/>
    <i>Vates grandisonam Vergilij tubam</i><br/>
    <i>Inflas, qua cecinit pium</i><br/>
    <i>Æneam Ausonij carminis alite,</i><br/>
    <i>Incenso quod ab Ilio</i><br/>
    <i>Hostes per medios telaque plurima</i><br/>
    <i>Grandaeuum eripuit patrem.</i><br/>
    <i>Seu docto gracilem pollice barbiton</i><br/>
    <i>Flacci concutis aemulus,</i><br/>
    <i>Lauro digna sonans carmina perpete.</i><br/>
    <i>Vrget strenua sed magis</i><br/>
    <i>Pectus cura tuum, teque per arduos</i><br/>
    <i>Pellit</i>
    </p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Marginal note at right "Pellit" is the catchword. Lines are verse; "Quod" and "Anyti" lines carry Greek-style capital initials and decorative swashes. Some letters uncertain in the italic type (e.g. "Mincigena", "Grandaeuum").</notes>
</page>
